Rearranging it into the standard form of a linear differential equation \(\frac{dy}{dx} + P(x)y = Q(x)\):
(1+x^2)\frac{dy}{dx} + xy = 5x^2\sqrt{1+x^2}
\frac{dy}{dx} + \frac{xy}{1+x^2} = \frac{5x^2\sqrt{1+x^2}}{1+x^2}
\frac{dy}{dx} + \frac{x}{1+x^2}y = \frac{5x^2}{\sqrt{1+x^2}}
Here, \(P(x) = \frac{x}{1+x^2}\) and \(Q(x) = \frac{5x^2}{\sqrt{1+x^2}}\).
The integrating factor (I.F.) is:
I.F. = e^{\int P(x)dx} = e^{\int \frac{x}{1+x^2}dx}
Let \(u = 1+x^2\), then \(du = 2xdx\), so \(xdx = \frac{1}{2}du\).
\int \frac{x}{1+x^2}dx = \int \frac{1}{2u}du = \frac{1}{2}\ln|u| = \frac{1}{2}\ln(1+x^2)
I.F. = e^{\frac{1}{2}\ln(1+x^2)} = e^{\ln((1+x^2)^{1/2})} = \sqrt{1+x^2}
The general solution is given by \(y \cdot (I.F.) = \int Q(x) \cdot (I.F.) dx + C\):
y\sqrt{1+x^2} = \int \frac{5x^2}{\sqrt{1+x^2}} \cdot \sqrt{1+x^2} dx + C
y\sqrt{1+x^2} = \int 5x^2 dx + C
y\sqrt{1+x^2} = \frac{5x^3}{3} + C
We are given the initial condition \(y(0)=0\). Substitute \(x=0\) and \(y=0\) into the general solution:
0\sqrt{1+0^2} = \frac{5(0)^3}{3} + C
0 = 0 + C \Rightarrow C = 0
So, the particular solution is:
y\sqrt{1+x^2} = \frac{5x^3}{3}
y = \frac{5x^3}{3\sqrt{1+x^2}}
Now, we need to find \(y(\sqrt{3})\). Substitute \(x=\sqrt{3}\) into the particular solution:
